    {
      "filename": "Flickr - ronsaunders47 - LOOKING TOWARDS THE LIVER BUILDING. LIVERPOOL. UK.jpg",
      "title": "File:Flickr - ronsaunders47 - LOOKING TOWARDS THE LIVER BUILDING. LIVERPOOL. UK.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Flickr_-_ronsaunders47_-_LOOKING_TOWARDS_THE_LIVER_BUILDING._LIVERPOOL._UK.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_-_ronsaunders47_-_LOOKING_TOWARDS_THE_LIVER_BUILDING._LIVERPOOL._UK.jpg",
      "width": 3648,
      "height": 2736,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Ronald Saunders from Warrington, UK",
      "description": "The Royal Liver Building  is a Grade I listed building located in Liverpool, England. It is sited at the Pier Head and along with the neighbouring Cunard Building and Port of Liverpool Building is one of Liverpool's Three Graces, which line the city's waterfront. It is also part of Liverpool's UNESCO designated World Heritage Maritime Mercantile City.\nOpened in 1911, the building is the purpose-built home of the Royal Liver Assurance group, which had been set up in the city in 1850 to provide locals with assistance related to losing a wage-earning relative. One of the first buildings in the world to be built using reinforced concrete, the Royal Liver Building stands at 90 m (300 ft) tall and was the tallest building in both Liverpool and the United Kingdom for over half a century.\n\nToday the Royal Liver Building is one of the most recognisable landmarks in the city of Liverpool and is home to two fabled Liver Birds that watch over the city and the sea. Legend has it that were these two birds to fly away, then the city would cease to exist.",
      "dateCreated": "2011-12-03 14:18",
